{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148189",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{144} & -\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{144} & \\frac{11}{144}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
